      {
        "id": "lem-qft-example-oscillator-feynman-prescription",
        "kind": "lemma",
        "domain": "mathematics",
        "title": "One-mode oscillator, Feynman boundary prescription, and retarded contrast",
        "statement": "Assume $\\mathrm{AC}_\\omega$ ([[def-countable-choice]]), $m_o>0$ in kilograms, $\\omega>0$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, and $\\hbar>0$ in $\\mathrm{J\\,s}$. Take the complete oscillator family $(\\psi_n)_{n\\ge0}$ and self-adjoint Hamiltonian $H_{\\rm osc}$ of [[thm-nrqm-oscillator-oscillator-scaling-and-exact-realization]], with oscillator length $\\ell=\\sqrt{\\hbar/(m_o\\omega)}$ and $H_{\\rm osc}\\psi_n=\\hbar\\omega(n+\\tfrac12)\\psi_n$. Let $\\mathcal C=\\operatorname{span}\\{\\psi_n:n\\ge0\\}$ be the finite Hermite span. Multiplication by position $Xf(x)=xf(x)$ maps $\\mathcal C$ into itself by the Hermite recurrence. Define $U(t)$ on $\\mathcal C$ by $U(t)\\psi_n=e^{-i\\omega(n+1/2)t}\\psi_n$ and extend continuously to a unitary on the oscillator Hilbert space. For $\\psi_0$ the normalized ground state, define\n$$C_F(t)=\\frac{m_o}{\\hbar}\\bigl(T X(t)X(0)\\psi_0,\\psi_0\\bigr)_1,\\qquad X(t)=U(t)^\\dagger XU(t),$$\nwhere time ordering means $T X(t)X(0)=X(t)X(0)$ for $t\\ge0$ and $X(0)X(t)$ for $t<0$. Define $G_R(t)=1_{[0,\\infty)}(t)\\sin(\\omega t)/\\omega$.\n\nThen, for every real $t$,\n$$C_F(t)=\\frac{e^{-i\\omega|t|}}{2\\omega},\\qquad (\\partial_t^2+\\omega^2)C_F=-i\\delta_0,\\qquad (\\partial_t^2+\\omega^2)G_R=\\delta_0.$$\nThe derivatives are distributional on $\\mathbb R$, and $\\delta_0(\\varphi)=\\varphi(0)$. Both kernels have units of seconds, so the differential identities have units of inverse seconds. In particular, $C_F(t)\\ne0$ for $t<0$ while $G_R(t)=0$ there.\n\nFor a Schwartz test function $\\varphi\\in\\mathcal S(\\mathbb R)$ and $\\epsilon>0$, define $\\operatorname{PV}\\int\\varphi(x)/x\\,dx:=\\lim_{r\\downarrow0}\\int_{|x|>r}\\varphi(x)/x\\,dx$ and $\\delta_a(\\varphi):=\\varphi(a)$. The Plemelj boundary identity is\n$$\\lim_{\\epsilon\\downarrow0}\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{\\varphi(x)}{x+i\\epsilon}\\,dx =\\operatorname{PV}\\int_{\\mathbb R}\\frac{\\varphi(x)}x\\,dx-i\\pi\\varphi(0),$$\nwith the opposite sign of $i\\epsilon$ reversing the delta sign. In this boundary formula $x$ and $\\epsilon$ use one common coordinate unit. For $\\nu\\in\\mathbb R$ in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$, the frequency regulator $\\epsilon>0$ is in $\\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and $\\epsilon t$ is dimensionless; the ordinary positive-sign Fourier transform of $e^{-\\epsilon|t|}C_F(t)$ is\n$$\\frac{i}{2\\omega}\\left(\\frac1{\\nu-\\omega+i\\epsilon}-\\frac1{\\nu+\\omega-i\\epsilon}\\right).$$\nAs $\\epsilon\\downarrow0$ its distributional limit is\n$$\\frac{i}{2\\omega}\\left[ \\operatorname{PV}\\frac1{\\nu-\\omega}-\\operatorname{PV}\\frac1{\\nu+\\omega} -i\\pi\\delta_{\\omega}-i\\pi\\delta_{-\\omega}\\right],$$\nwhich is conventionally denoted $i/(\\nu^2-\\omega^2+i0)$. Here that notation means the displayed pair of opposite boundary values at the two simple poles. This one-mode result makes no spatial-causality or interacting-field claim.",
        "deps": [
          "def-qft-example-data",
          "def-complex-exponential",
          "def-countable-choice",
          "def-fourier-transform-on-l-one-of-rn",
          "def-schwartz-space-and-its-seminorms",
          "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "lem-l-two-with-the-integral-pairing-is-a-hilbert-space",
          "thm-nrqm-oscillator-oscillator-scaling-and-exact-realization",
          "thm-complex-exponential-is-entire-with-derivative-itself",
          "thm-fubini-theorem-for-l-one-on-sigma-finite-product-spaces",
          "thm-fourier-transform-maps-schwartz-space-continuously-to-itself",
          "thm-nrqm-oscillator-hermite-recurrence"
        ],
